 "transcript": {
  "answer": "Yes. It's getting harder to find work in Queensland, though the change is gradual rather than sudden.\n\n**Unemployment is rising (ABS Labour Force, September 2026)**\n- The seasonally adjusted rate was **4.53%** in September 2026. That's the highest in the two years of data I looked at, and up from 4.17% in September 2025 and 3.98% in September 2024.\n- About **144,400 Queenslanders were unemployed**. That's roughly 14,000 more than a year earlier and 22,000 more than two years ago.\n- Queensland's monthly figures jump around a lot. For example, they read 3.75% in March and 3.73% in May, then 4.3–4.5% in the months around them. The trend series smooths this out, and it shows the same direction: unemployment fell slightly to **4.11% in February 2026**, then rose steadily to **4.47% in September 2026**.\n\n**More people are looking than there are new jobs**\n- Employment is still growing, with about **3.05 million employed** in September 2026 (trend). But growth is slowing, from about 6,000–7,000 extra jobs a month in early 2026 to about 2,000–3,000 a month since July.\n- The share of working-age people in jobs fell from 64.3% in September 2024 to **63.9%** in September 2026.\n- Participation, meaning the share of people working or looking for work, has stayed at about **66.9%**. So the rise in unemployment isn't from more people entering the job market. Jobs just aren't keeping up with population growth.\n\n**Compared with the rest of Australia**\nQueensland is still slightly better off than the national average. Australia's unemployment rate was **4.72%** in September 2026, up from 4.43% a year earlier, and the national trend is weakening in a similar way.\n\n**Overall:** Queensland's job market is softening, not collapsing. Unemployment is still low by historical standards, but it has risen by about half a percentage point over the past year. It's also climbing faster since mid-2026, so job seekers are facing more competition than they did a year or two ago.",
